#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185310 :tutorial/content "The product rule for radicals also works for higher-order roots, like the cube root. In the case of the cube root, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{ab} = \\sqrt[3]{a}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{b}\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{ab} = \\sqrt[n]{a}\\cdot \\sqrt[n]{b}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f9406c08-e6de-514e-aeae-9f581d5730a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1607 :tutorial/title "The Product Rule for Cube Roots"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185346 :tutorial/content "The **product rule for radicals** states that the square root of a product equals the product of the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{a} \\cdot \\sqrt{b} = \\sqrt{ab} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the product rule to combine radical expressions.\n\nFor example, let's suppose we want to combine the radicals in the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5x} \\cdot \\sqrt{6z}\n$$\n\nTo combine the radicals, we apply the product rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{5x} \\cdot \\sqrt{6z} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{5x \\cdot 6z} &=  \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{30xz}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1c9d8f0d-315b-5c83-a8e6-17638c1c3d24" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6200 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
